@anon.53fe85e3a453dfbf0ab1e8743506e67c.1367 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [3839 x i8] }> <{ [3839 x i8] c"A mapping from argument values to a return value.\0A\0AYou can call a function by writing a comma-separated list of function\0A_arguments_ enclosed in parentheses directly after the function name.\0AAdditionally, you can pass any number of trailing content blocks arguments\0Ato a function _after_ the normal argument list. If the normal argument list\0Awould become empty, it can be omitted. Typst supports positional and named\0Aarguments. The former are identified by position and type, while the later\0Aare written as `name: value`.\0A\0AWithin math mode, function calls have special behaviour. See the\0A[math documentation]($category/math) for more details.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A// Call a function.\0A#list([A], [B])\0A\0A// Named arguments and trailing\0A// content blocks.\0A#enum(start: 2)[A][B]\0A\0A// Version without parentheses.\0A#list[A][B]\0A```\0A\0AFunctions are a fundamental building block of Typst. Typst provides\0Afunctions for a variety of typesetting tasks. Moreover, the markup you write\0Ais backed by functions and all styling happens through functions. This\0Areference lists all available functions and how you can use them. Please\0Aalso refer to the documentation about [set]($styling/#set-rules) and\0A[show]($styling/#show-rules) rules to learn about additional ways you can\0Awork with functions in Typst.\0A\0A# Element functions\0ASome functions are associated with _elements_ like [headings]($heading) or\0A[tables]($table). When called, these create an element of their respective\0Akind. In contrast to normal functions, they can further be used in [set\0Arules]($styling/#set-rules), [show rules]($styling/#show-rules), and\0A[selectors]($selector).\0A\0A# Function scopes\0AFunctions can hold related definitions in their own scope, similar to a\0A[module]($scripting/#modules). Examples of this are\0A[`assert.eq`]($assert.eq) or [`list.item`]($list.item). However, this\0Afeature is currently only available for built-in functions.\0A\0A# Defining functions\0AYou can define your own function with a [let binding]($scripting/#bindings)\0Athat has a parameter list after the binding's name. The parameter list can\0Acontain mandatory positional parameters, named parameters with default\0Avalues and [argument sinks]($arguments).\0A\0AThe right-hand side of a function binding is the function body, which can be\0Aa block or any other expression. It defines the function's return value and\0Acan depend on the parameters. If the function body is a [code\0Ablock]($scripting/#blocks), the return value is the result of joining the\0Avalues of each expression in the block.\0A\0AWithin a function body, the `return` keyword can be used to exit early and\0Aoptionally specify a return value. If no explicit return value is given, the\0Abody evaluates to the result of joining all expressions preceding the\0A`return`.\0A\0A```example\0A#let alert(body, fill: red) = {\0A  set text(white)\0A  set align(center)\0A  rect(\0A    fill: fill,\0A    inset: 8pt,\0A    radius: 4pt,\0A    [*Warning:\\ #body*],\0A  )\0A}\0A\0A#alert[\0A  Danger is imminent!\0A]\0A\0A#alert(fill: blue)[\0A  KEEP OFF TRACKS\0A]\0A```\0A\0A# Unnamed functions { #unnamed }\0AYou can also created an unnamed function without creating a binding by\0Aspecifying a parameter list followed by `=>` and the function body. If your\0Afunction has just one parameter, the parentheses around the parameter list\0Aare optional. Unnamed functions are mainly useful for show rules, but also\0Afor settable properties that take functions like the page function's\0A[`footer`]($page.footer) property.\0A\0A```example\0A#show \22once?\22: it => [#it #it]\0Aonce?\0A```\0A\0A# Note on function purity\0AIn Typst, all functions are _pure._ This means that for the same\0Aarguments, they always return the same result. They cannot \22remember\22 things to\0Aproduce another value when they are called a second time.\0A\0AThe only exception are built-in methods like\0A[`array.push(value)`]($array.push). These can modify the values they are\0Acalled on." }>, align 1
